   {
    "__component": "sections.columns",
    "alias": "columns",
    "styles": null,
    "columns": [
     {
      "text": [
       {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "children": [
         {
          "bold": true,
          "text": "Hidden patterns",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
         {
          "text": "",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
         {
          "text": "Systemic facilitation has its roots in systemic theory and in the constellations developed by Bert Hellinger, which reveal hidden patterns in human and organizational relationships. This methodology is fundamental to address conflicts and tensions within social systems, as it allows understanding the dynamics of power, hidden hierarchies and interpersonal connections that often remain out of sight. Franz Ruppert expanded this work by applying constellations to the organizational field, exploring how cultural and historical patterns influence the health of collective systems.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
         {
          "text": "",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
         {
          "text": "Insa Sparrer, with his innovations in the application of constellations, has shown how this technique can make visible what is invisible within an organization. According to Sparrer, human systems are influenced by complex dynamics that often remain hidden, but can be unraveled by tools such as systemic constellations. This allows to unlock latent potentials and create a path towards genuine collaboration and internal harmony.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
         {
          "text": "",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
         {
          "text": "In academia, systemic facilitation is also based on the idea of systemic consciousness, a central concept to understand how systems can evolve in a healthy way. This awareness involves a holistic look that integrates personal relationships, cultural values and structural dynamics to generate sustainable solutions. Thus systemic facilitation has become a critical tool for organizational and community transformation in complex and changing contexts.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      "title": null,
      "titleHeading": null,
